# •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••
# 🎥 FRIGATE — ENVIRONMENT (EXAMPLE) 🎥
# •••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••••

# Copy to .env and edit. This is the only file you MUST touch to move this
# folder to a different host; config/config.yml is the only other file you will
# normally edit, and that one describes cameras rather than hardware.
#
#   cp .env.example .env && $EDITOR .env
#
# Camera passwords go here. config.yml references them as
# {FRIGATE_RTSP_PASSWORD}, so the file you paste into a lab document stays
# clean. .env is gitignored; .env.example is not.

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                              🧩 PROFILE SELECTION
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# The whole portability story is this one line. Compose reads COMPOSE_FILE out
# of .env by itself, so `docker compose up -d` needs no -f flags.
#
# Pick exactly one NETWORK overlay and exactly one DETECTOR overlay:
#
#   network   compose.wireguard.yaml ..... tunnel sidecar (DEFAULT)
#             compose.ports.yaml ......... no tunnel on this host
#
#   detector  compose.openvino.yaml ...... Intel iGPU/NPU — LattePanda
#             compose.coral-usb.yaml ..... Coral USB — works on every host here
#             compose.hailo.yaml ......... Pi 5 + AI HAT+ (M.2 Hailo module)
#             compose.rpi.yaml ........... Pi 4, hardware decode, CPU detect
#             compose.cpu.yaml ........... no accelerator anywhere
#
# The WireGuard overlay is the default because the normal deployment ingests
# from BOTH local LAN cameras and cameras across a tunnel, at the same time.
# It handles both: the tunnel is split, so only the prefixes named in
# wireguard/wg0.conf go through it and everything else — LAN cameras, the
# broker, replies to LAN browsers — takes the host's ordinary route.
#
# Switch to compose.ports.yaml on a host with no remote cameras at all. That
# host then needs no wireguard/wg0.conf.
#
# Whatever you pick, uncomment the matching detector block in config/config.yml.
# The two have to agree; nothing checks that for you except frigate-setup.sh.
#
COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.wireguard.yaml:compose.openvino.yaml
#COMPOSE_FILE=compose.yaml:compose.ports.yaml:compose.openvino.yaml

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                          🐳 IMAGE
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# Pinned deliberately. `stable` moves under you, and on an NVR the symptom of
# an unwanted upgrade is footage you do not have.
#
#   amd64 (LattePanda, any x86) ........ ghcr.io/blakeblackshear/frigate:0.17.2
#   Raspberry Pi 4 / 5 (arm64) ......... ghcr.io/blakeblackshear/frigate:0.17.2
#   other arm64 (Gateworks Venice) ..... ghcr.io/blakeblackshear/frigate:0.17.2-standard-arm64
#
# On arm64 the plain tag is the Raspberry-Pi-optimized build. A Gateworks
# Venice board is generic arm64 and wants -standard-arm64.
#
FRIGATE_IMAGE=ghcr.io/blakeblackshear/frigate:0.17.2

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                        💾 STORAGE
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# Where recordings live, on the HOST. Bind-mounted to /media/frigate.
#
# Put this on an external disk. Continuous recording writes constantly, and an
# SD card will fail in months — not a theoretical risk, the ordinary outcome.
# A USB SSD is the cheapest fix there is.
#
#   sudo mkdir -p /mnt/frigate && sudo chown -R 1000:1000 /mnt/frigate
#
FRIGATE_MEDIA=/mnt/frigate

# Recording cache, as tmpfs, in BYTES. Segments are assembled here and moved to
# FRIGATE_MEDIA when complete — RAM instead of a second write of every recorded
# byte. Roughly 100 MB per camera; 1 GB covers a typical eight-camera box.
FRIGATE_CACHE_SIZE=1000000000

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                  🧠 SHARED MEMORY
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# Frigate decodes into /dev/shm. Docker's 64 MB default is not enough for one
# camera and fails as decode stalls, not as an obvious error.
#
#   per camera MB = (detect_width * detect_height * 1.5 * 20 + 270480) / 1048576
#
# At the 640x480 detect resolution this stack recommends, that is ~9 MB per
# camera. Sum them, add 40 MB for birdseye, then round generously upward — the
# cost of over-allocating is reserved RAM, the cost of under-allocating is
# cameras that silently stop.
FRIGATE_SHM_SIZE=256mb

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                    🔐 CREDENTIALS
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# The RTSP credentials Frigate uses to pull from mediamtx. Referenced in
# config.yml as {FRIGATE_RTSP_USER} and {FRIGATE_RTSP_PASSWORD}.
#
# These are mediamtx's users, created by ~/projects/docker-containers/mediamtx/
# mediamtx-users.sh. A read-only user is enough; Frigate never publishes.
#
# Leave both empty if mediamtx is running unauthenticated, and strip the
# `user:pass@` from the camera paths in config.yml.
FRIGATE_RTSP_USER=
FRIGATE_RTSP_PASSWORD=

# Signs Frigate's session cookies. Minimum 64 characters, and it must stay the
# same across restarts or every user is logged out on every `up`.
# frigate-setup.sh generates one and writes it here if you leave it empty.
#
#   openssl rand -hex 32
FRIGATE_JWT_SECRET=

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                🌐 PORTS & BINDING
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# Change the BIND values to pin a service to one interface. The defaults listen
# everywhere, which is right for a dedicated NVR on a trusted segment and wrong
# for a host with a leg on an untrusted one.
#
# 8971 is the authenticated, TLS-wrapped UI. It is the only one you should
# expose. Port 5000 — the unauthenticated API — is not published at all; see
# the comment in compose.ports.yaml before you change that.
FRIGATE_UI_BIND=0.0.0.0
FRIGATE_UI_PORT=8971

# RTSP restream out of go2rtc. Note 8554 is also mediamtx's RTSP port: if this
# box ALSO runs mediamtx, change one of them or they will not both bind.
FRIGATE_RTSP_BIND=0.0.0.0
FRIGATE_RTSP_PORT=8554

# WebRTC for low-latency live view. TCP and UDP both.
FRIGATE_WEBRTC_BIND=0.0.0.0
FRIGATE_WEBRTC_PORT=8555

# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
#                                                                                  🖥 HOST SPECIFICS
# ⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼⎼
# Timestamps in the UI and in recording filenames.
TZ=America/New_York

# OpenVINO profile only. The host's numeric `render` group, so the container
# can open /dev/dri/renderD128. Names do not resolve across the boundary.
#   getent group render | cut -d: -f3
#
# Left empty on purpose — there is no portable default (Debian 12 ~104-106,
# Ubuntu 22.04 ~110, Ubuntu 24.04 993). frigate-setup.sh detects and writes it.
# Until it is set, the openvino profile fails to start with a message saying so.
RENDER_GID=

# Intel VAAPI driver name. LEAVE THIS COMMENTED OUT on anything modern and let
# libva auto-detect — gen8 and later (including Raptor Lake) want iHD, which
# auto-detection picks on its own.
#
# Commented is NOT the same as `LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=`. An empty assignment still
# puts the variable in the container, which stops libva auto-detecting and
# makes it dlopen `_drv_video.so`; vaInitialize then fails outright and EVERY
# hwaccel path dies. Assign a value only to override:
#   iHD  — gen8+ (the auto-detected choice; no reason to set it by hand)
#   i965 — roughly pre-gen8, only if hardware decoding fails without it
#LIBVA_DRIVER_NAME=iHD

# WireGuard profile only. Ownership of the sidecar's config directory.
PUID=1000
PGID=1000
